# hostile plan: seed=0 ops=8
#
# THE RECHUNK CLen/Alg METADATA BUG, minimized. Found by the owner during
# release week and by no gate, and the fix commit says exactly why:
#
#     Sealer.rechunk filled CLen in from len(c.Data) and left Alg and
#     KeyID at zero, so every row a re-chunk emitted claimed the entry in
#     the pack was the size of its decoded bytes and needed no decoding.
#     That is true only for bytes zstd could not shrink [...]
#     Nothing caught it because every test that re-chunks used
#     incompressible pseudorandom bodies.
#
# The exerciser had the same hole. Every op it generated wrote
# incompressible bytes on purpose -- so the packer had real work and
# nothing deduped away -- and incompressible bytes are the ONE case where
# a row copied from the plaintext happens to be right: zstd gives up, the
# entry is stored verbatim, and CLen == LLen. `fill=text` and `fill=zero`
# are what closed it.
#
# THE SHAPE, and every line of it is load-bearing:
#
#   1. a body zstd can shrink. fill=text compresses ~3.6x under the
#      volume's own settings, so the entry in the pack is nothing like
#      the length of the plaintext.
#   2. a CHECKPOINT. A seal only takes the re-chunk path for a piece that
#      covers part of a chunk ALREADY IN A PACK. Bytes still in the ring
#      are chunked whole by the flush path, which was never wrong. The
#      settle is what turns the create into a packed chunk.
#   3. a partial overwrite STRICTLY INSIDE the file. Not at 0 and not
#      past the end: either of those replaces or appends whole chunks and
#      re-chunks nothing. The chunk has to straddle the rewrite.
#   4. a second overwrite with the OTHER compressible kind, so the span
#      recompresses to a different length than it did the first time --
#      which is the number the row has to carry from the entry rather
#      than from the bytes in hand.
#
# WHERE IT SHOWS. Not in the live view: the mount serves these bytes out
# of its own overlay and gets them right. It shows in phase C, on a cold
# mount of the SEALED generation, where a reader follows the row's CLen
# to a range that is not the entry -- so this entry, like the sparse-train
# one, is a reason the lifecycle phases are not optional.
#
# ON AN ENCRYPTED VOLUME this reproduces for ANY fill, compressible or
# not: a nonce and a GCM tag are always added, so no entry is ever its
# plaintext's length. `scripts/hostile-docker.sh --encrypt` is that leg.
#
# Fixed at 2afa231. PRESENT at its parent, c26428f.
mkdir rechunk mode=0755
create rechunk/base.log len=40000 fill=text:11 # a body zstd shrinks ~3.6x
settle ms=1100 # the checkpoint that packs it; without this nothing re-chunks
pwrite rechunk/base.log off=7001 len=9001 fill=text:22 # strictly inside: the chunk straddles it
pwrite rechunk/base.log off=21001 len=6001 fill=zero # recompresses to a different length again
create rechunk/plain.dat len=40000 fill=41 # the control: incompressible, CLen == LLen
settle ms=1100
pwrite rechunk/plain.dat off=7001 len=9001 fill=42 # the same shape that never showed the bug
compare
